ts every filmmaker to"},{"sec":3.6,"text":"reproduce Valmiki's Ramayana word to"},{"sec":6,"text":"word. Creative freedom is part of the"},{"sec":7.9,"text":"tradition. Countless poets brought their"},{"sec":10.3,"text":"own interpretation to the epic. They"},{"sec":12,"text":"added their own creativity, their own"},{"sec":13.8,"text":"literary style, and their own"},{"sec":15.3,"text":"perspective. But they never abandoned"},{"sec":17.5,"text":"the moral heart of the story. That"},{"sec":19.4,"text":"reading of Ravana does not come from"},{"sec":21.3,"text":"Kamban or Tulsidas. It comes from"},{"sec":23.4,"text":"left-leaning retelling of the epic. When"},{"sec":25.3,"text":"cinematic adoption does that, it raises"},{"sec":27.6,"text":"a bigger question. Have we changed the"},{"sec":29.3,"text":"way the story is told or have we changed"},{"sec":31.6,"text":"what the story is trying to say? The"},{"sec":33.4,"text":"Ramayana survived because every"},{"sec":34.9,"text":"generation found a way to tell it while"},{"sec":36.9,"text":"preserving its central moral conflict."},{"sec":39,"text":"Today's cinema has that responsibility"},{"sec":40.9,"text":"because how we tell Ramayana now will"},{"sec":43.2,"text":"shape what the next generation"},{"sec":44.6,"text":"understands about it."}],"text":""}}